# Inkling UI Standards

Apply these rules to every UI change.

## Mobile first

- Design for narrow phone screens before adding tablet and desktop enhancements.
- Keep layouts usable at 320px wide; avoid fixed widths that cause horizontal scrolling.
- Use responsive spacing, wrapping, and stacking for controls, dialogs, cards, and headers.
- Make touch targets at least 44px where practical, and preserve visible focus states.
- Test important flows at mobile and desktop widths before considering the UI complete.

## Markdown and rich content safety

- Never let Markdown, code blocks, tables, long URLs, images, or KaTeX math overflow their container.
- Preserve the existing Markdown, GFM, raw HTML sanitization, syntax, and math-rendering behavior.
- Use constrained responsive images, horizontal scrolling only for inherently wide content such as tables/code, and wrapping for prose.
- Check rendered content with long headings, nested lists, wide tables, multiline code, inline math, display math, and missing images.
- Do not hide or truncate note content merely to solve overflow.

## Color system

Use one Inkling black-and-white color scheme consistently; do not introduce unrelated accent colors.

- App background: `#0f172a` (slate 900)
- Surface/card: `#1e293b` (slate 800)
- Elevated surface/input: `#334155` (slate 700)
- Primary accent/action: `#f8fafc` (slate 50)
- Primary hover/focus: `#ffffff` (white)
- Main text: `#f8fafc` (slate 50)
- Muted text/borders: `#94a3b8` / `#475569`
- Use grayscale for normal UI states. Semantic success, warning, and error may use accessible tones only when communicating a real status.

Prefer existing design tokens or shared classes over one-off color values. Check contrast for text, controls, disabled states, and focus indicators.
